<GuideLayout title="#57 Stone: Size, Fines and Ordering Checks" metaDescription="Read a #57 product as a grading designation, then check its actual stone type, fines, washing and loose bulk weight." canonicalPath="/57-stone-size-guide" guideTitle="#57 Stone: Size, Fines and Ordering Checks" guideSubtitle="Read a #57 product as a grading designation, then check its actual stone type, fines, washing and loose bulk weight." standardBadge="Read the actual product specification" readingTime="4 min read" lastUpdated="November 1, 2026" calculatorsSupported={calculatorsSupported}>

<section aria-labelledby="meaning"><h2 id="meaning">What the #57 number tells you</h2>
<p>#57 identifies an aggregate size grading in the specification being used. It is not a 57-millimeter diameter, a mineral name, a test of cleanliness or a universal weight per yard. A quarry may produce a crushed #57 stone; another supplier may sell a rounded gravel meeting a specified #57 grading.</p>
<p>Use the number to start a product conversation. Obtain the full designation and applicable specification, then match it to the layer shown in the project detail. The <a href="/gravel-sizes">gravel size guide</a> explains nominal sizes, numbered sieves and regional product names.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="read-report"><h2 id="read-report">Read the sieve result as percentages</h2>
<p>A gradation report lists sieve openings and the percentage of the dry sample passing each opening. A nominal range is a short description; compliance is evaluated against the applicable limits across the required sieves.</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table><caption>How to read a grading report without treating its label as a complete product specification</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Report field</th><th scope="col">What it describes</th><th scope="col">What to ask</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Nominal size</th><td>A shorthand for the grading</td><td>Which specification and size designation apply?</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Sieve opening</th><td>The opening through which a fraction of the sample passes</td><td>Are inch and numbered-sieve values being compared correctly?</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Percent passing</th><td>The dry sample fraction passing that opening</td><td>Does the complete result meet the project’s limits?</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Fines result</th><td>The measured small-particle fraction at the specified sieve</td><td>Does that result and the wash status suit the layer?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For example, if one required sieve permits 0–10% passing and a sample reports 5%, that sieve result is within the stated band. It does not establish compliance at the other sieves, product durability, washing or suitability for a drain. A No. 8 sieve result is not the same measurement as the finer No. 200 sieve.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="properties"><h2 id="properties">Confirm the properties the number leaves out</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Source and shape:</strong> mineral type and crushed or rounded particles.</li><li><strong>Fines and washing:</strong> use actual product information, rather than assuming every #57 load is washed or has zero fines.</li><li><strong>Loose bulk weight:</strong> a quoted or measured pounds-per-yard value for the product being sold.</li><li><strong>Project use:</strong> the specified layer, filter or bedding, and placement requirements.</li></ul>
<p>A scale ticket can document delivered weight. A sieve analysis describes grading. Neither substitutes for the other. <a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/penndot/programs-and-doing-business/construction/aggregate-plant-book">PennDOT’s Aggregate Plant Book</a> provides separate gradation, tonnage and quality-control records, illustrating why these attributes must be checked separately.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="quantity"><h2 id="quantity">Estimate the selected #57 layer</h2>
<p>Once the product and thickness are selected, use area × depth in inches ÷ 324 for cubic yards. A 100 sq ft layer at 3 inches occupies 0.93 yd³ before extra. With a chosen 10% allowance, it needs 1.02 yd³. At an explicitly assumed 1.35 US short tons per loose yard, that is about 1.38 tons; another supplier weight changes the ton result.</p>
<p>For a pipe trench, use the <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain rock calculator</a> to account for cover and pipe displacement. For a general order, use the <a href="/crushed-stone-calculator">crushed stone calculator</a>. Calculate a separate base or surface as its own material.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="reference"><h2 id="reference">Use the governing specification for the job</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/penndot/documents/public/pubsforms/publications/pub_408/408_2026/408_2026_ie.pdf">PennDOT Publication 408, 2026 edition</a> and the <a href="https://www.vdot.virginia.gov/doing-business/technical-guidance-and-support/technical-guidance-documents/road-and-bridge-specifications/">VDOT specification library</a> are examples of agency-specific source documents. Apply the edition, supplements and project requirements that govern your work. This guide explains reading and ordering; it does not approve an aggregate source or select a drainage design.</p></section>

